      "text": "Given the matrix \\(\\mathbf{P}\\) representing the projection onto the vector \\(\\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix}\\), we need to find \\(\\mathbf{P}^{-1}\\). To determine whether \\(\\mathbf{P}^{-1}\\) exists, we need to check the determinant of \\(\\mathbf{P}\\).\n\nThe expression for the projection matrix \\(\\mathbf{P}\\) from a vector \\(\\mathbf{v}\\) onto a vector \\(\\mathbf{u}\\) is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{P} = \\frac{\\mathbf{u} \\mathbf{u}^T}{\\mathbf{u}^T \\mathbf{u}}\n\\]\n\nFor the vector \\(\\mathbf{u} = \\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix}\\), compute \\(\\mathbf{P}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{u} \\mathbf{u}^T = \\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} -3 & -2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 4 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nCalculate \\(\\mathbf{u}^T \\mathbf{u}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{u}^T \\mathbf{u} = (-3)(-3) + (-2)(-2) = 9 + 4 = 13\n\\]\n\nThus,\n\\[\n\\mathbf{P} = \\frac{1}{13} \\begin{pmatrix} 9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 4 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{9}{13} & \\frac{6}{13} \\\\ \\frac{6}{13} & \\frac{4}{13} \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nTo find the inverse of \\(\\mathbf{P}\\), we applylehmann's formula to handle the eigenvalues computation involving \\(\\mathbf{u}\\). Generally we look for the determinant of \\mathbf{P}:\n\n\\[\n|\\mathbf{P}| = \\frac{1}{13} \\left| \\begin{vmatrix} 9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 4 \\end{vmatrix} \\right| = \\frac{1}{13} \\left| (4)(9) - (6)(6)\\right| = \\frac{1}{13} (36 - 36\n= 0\n\nSince \\(\\left| \\mathbf{P} \\right| = 0\\), the projection matrix \\(\\mathbf{P}\\) is not invertible. Hence,\n\\[\n\\left( \\mathbf{P} \\right)^{-1} \\text{ does not exist, and we write the zero matrix.}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{0}\n\\]",
