      "text": "To find the matrix \\(\\mathbf{P}\\) that projects any vector onto the vector \\(\\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix}\\), we can use the standard formula for projecting any vector onto another vector:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{P}\\mathbf{v} = \\frac{\\mathbf{v} \\cdot \\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix}}{\\left\\| \\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix} \\right\\|^2} \\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nFirst let’s compute the magnitude of \\(\\mathbf{v}'\\).\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left\\| \\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix} \\right\\| &= \\sqrt{(-3)^2 + (-2)^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{9 + 4} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{13} .\\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nNow, we can use this magnitude to compute \\(\\mathbf{P}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{P} \\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix} &= \\frac{\\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix}}{\\left\\| \\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix} \\right\\|^2} \\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{(-3) \\cdot (-3) + (-2) \\cdot (-2)}{13} \\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{9 + 4}{13} \\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{13}{13}  \\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThus, the mapping \\(\\mathbf{P}\\mathbf{v}\\) represents projection onto a vector \\(\\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\nTo find \\(\\mathbf{P}^{-1}\\), observe that \\(\\mathbf{P}\\) is a projection matrix, meaning \\(\\mathbf{P}^2 = \\mathbf{P}\\). Also, if \\(\\mathbf{v}'\\) has an orthogonal basis vector \\(\\begin{pmatrix} v_1’ \\\\ v_2 \\end{pmatrix}\\) where two variables are orthogonal, \\(\\mathbf{P}\\) projects onto one of those basis vectors.\n\nIn our case, the vector \\(\\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix}\\) is an eigenvector of the projection matrix. Specifically, \\(\\mathbf{P}\\mathbf{v}' = \\mu \\mathbf{v}'\\) where \\(\\mu = \\frac{3}{13}\\).\n\nSince it is an \\(2 \\times 2\\) matrix and because it equals its own inverse under inversion:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{P}^{-1} = \\mathbf{P}\n\\]\n\nWe get:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{pmatrix}\n\\frac{3}{13} & \\frac{-4}{13} \\\\\n\\frac{-4}{13} & \\frac{11}{13}\n\\end{pmatrix}\n}\n\\]\nis the correct answer for the inverse of matrix \\(\\mathbf{P}\\), being a valid projection matrix \\(\\mathbf{P}^{-1}.\\).",
