      "text": "First, find the projection matrix for projecting onto the vector $\\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix}$ by observing the steps in my handwritten solution. Then, subtract the projection matrix from a diagonal matrix with entries of $\\frac{1}{3}$ and $\\frac{1}{2}$.\n\\begin{align*}\n\\mathbf{P} &= \\frac{1}{3^2 + 2^2} \\, \\mathbf{A} \\, \\mathbf{A}^T \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3^2 + 2^2} \\left( \\begin{pmatrix} -3 & -2 \\end{pmatrix} \\, \\begin{pmatrix} -3 & -2 \\end{pmatrix} \\right) \\\\\n&= \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{13} & \\frac{2}{13} \\\\ \\frac{2}{13} & \\frac{2}{13} \\end{pmatrix}\n\\end{align*}\nThrow away the upper-right entry.\n\\begin{align*}\n\\mathbf{P} - \\mathbf{I} &= \\begin{pmatrix} -\\frac{12}{13} \\\\ -\\frac{10}{13} \\end{pmatrix}\n\\end{align*}\nCan you find the inverse of this matrix? Eventually, you will have....\n\\begin{align*}\n\\mathbf{P}^{-1} &= \\boxed{\\text{zero matrix}}\n\\end{align*}\nNote: This problem was adapted from Anderson, et al., Problem Books from the MIT Mathematics Department, Vol. 2.\nLet \\(\\mathbf{v} = \\begin{pmatrix} {-3} \\\\ {-2} \\end{pmatrix}\\) be the vector we wish to project onto. This is the row vector because projections, up to isomorphism, are column transformations.\n\nWith the information that we have up here, we can use all of the standard column transformation rules to set up a real-life projection problem.\n\nThe first step is to add one to the second entry in our projection matrix. This gives a projection of \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix}\\) onto the row vector \\(\\mathbf{v}\\), giving a projection matrix of\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\mathbf{P}' &= \\frac{1}{3^2 + 2^2} \\, \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix} \\, \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{13} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\end{align*}\n\nLetting us know that the projection matrix we need is the upper-left entry of the generalized decomposition for our projection matrix, we have\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\mathbf{P} &= \\frac{1}{13} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4 \\end{pmatrix} \\end{align*}\n\nwhich is a projection onto the vector \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\nWe are now given the task of finding the inverse of a matrix. This is not possible, however, because this matrix is singular.\n\nWe are, of course, going to have to use more vectors in order to find the projection onto a different direction. This gives us another choice. We can use what we know about binary operations with matrices, we will get a similar answer.",
