      "text": "To find the projection matrix \\(\\mathbf{P}\\) that projects any vector onto the vector \\(\\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix}\\), we use the formula for the projection matrix onto a vector \\(\\mathbf{a}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{P} = \\frac{\\mathbf{a} \\mathbf{a}^T}{\\mathbf{a}^T \\mathbf{a}}\n\\]\n\nHere, \\(\\mathbf{a} = \\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix}\\). Thus, we need to compute \\(\\mathbf{a}^T \\mathbf{a}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{a}^T \\mathbf{a} = (-3 \\cdot -3) + (-2 \\cdot -2) = 9 + 4 = 13\n\\]\n\nNext, we find \\(\\mathbf{a} \\mathbf{a}^T\\):\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{a} \\mathbf{a}^T = \\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} -3 & -2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 4 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the projection matrix \\(\\mathbf{P}\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{P} = \\frac{1}{13} \\begin{pmatrix} 9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 4 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nThe inverse of a \\(2 \\times 2\\) matrix \\(\\mathbf{A} = \\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ c & d \\end{pmatrix}\\) is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A}^{-1} = \\frac{1}{ad - bc} \\begin{pmatrix} d & -b \\\\ -c & a \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(\\mathbf{P}\\), we have \\(a = 9\\), \\(b = 6\\), \\(c = 6\\), and \\(d = 4\\). Thus,\n\n\\[\nad - bc = (9)(4) - (6)(6) = 36 - 36 = 0\n\\]\n\nSince \\(ad - bc = 0\\), the inverse of \\(\\mathbf{P}\\) does not exist. As the problem states, we enter the zero matrix. Therefore, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\end{pmatrix}}\n\\]",
